WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.660
In the mouse cortex, the first cortical plate neurons end up deepest, beside the

2
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.540
white matter. Neurons born days later end up near the surface.

3
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.340
Yet these neurons are made near the ventricle, below.

4
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.560
So the later neurons must climb past the cortical plate neurons made before them.

5
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.440
How does a neuron's birthday decide where it settles?

6
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.930
Progenitors line the ventricle.

7
00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.030
They divide, and the new neurons travel outward.

8
00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.190
The first to leave form a thin sheet under the outer surface, the preplate.

9
00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:39.270
The next wave settles inside the preplate and splits it in two.

10
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.630
The outer part is the marginal zone.

11
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.790
The inner part is the subplate.

12
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:48.610
Each later wave passes the neurons already there and settles above them.

13
00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.530
Layer 6 is made first.

14
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.650
Layer 2 is made last.

15
00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:55.243
How do you know when a neuron was born?

16
00:00:55.763 --> 00:00:58.763
You date its last S phase, when DNA is copied.

17
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:03.823
Give a pregnant mouse one dose of thymidine carrying tritium, a radioactive form of

18
00:01:03.823 --> 00:01:08.703
hydrogen. Only cells copying DNA in the next hour or so are marked.

19
00:01:09.163 --> 00:01:11.323
A cell that divides again halves its label.

20
00:01:11.703 --> 00:01:15.863
So a neuron that stays heavily labeled made its last copy while the label was there.

21
00:01:16.323 --> 00:01:17.543
The label marks that moment.

22
00:01:18.063 --> 00:01:19.123
It tells the cell nothing.

23
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:26.473
In 1961, Jay Angevine and Richard Sidman gave pulses on different embryonic days.

24
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:29.513
Cells labeled earliest lay deepest.

25
00:01:30.273 --> 00:01:32.993
Cells labeled on later days lay nearer the surface.

26
00:01:33.813 --> 00:01:35.533
This ruled out a simple idea.

27
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:41.453
If new cells just shoved older ones outward, the earliest cells would end up on top.

28
00:01:41.893 --> 00:01:42.933
They ended up on the bottom.

29
00:01:43.653 --> 00:01:44.913
Later cells passed them.

30
00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:49.907
Does the progenitor carry its own schedule, or do its surroundings tell its daughter

31
00:01:49.907 --> 00:01:54.867
where to go? Susan McConnell moved ferret progenitors that make deep -layer neurons

32
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.127
into an older host.

33
00:01:56.907 --> 00:02:01.427
Cells moved while copying DNA mostly settled in the upper layers, like the host's

34
00:02:01.427 --> 00:02:06.427
own neurons. Cells moved later in the cycle mostly settled in the deep layers.

35
00:02:06.987 --> 00:02:08.467
Their fate was already fixed.

36
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:12.487
Late progenitors put into a young host still made upper layer neurons.

37
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.990
So a neuron's birthday predicts its layer.

38
00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:18.090
Early neurons sit deep.

39
00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.930
Later neurons pass them and settle above.

40
00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:26.630
In ferret experiments, a progenitor's choices narrow with time, and they do not

41
00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:32.390
reverse. This is a strong tendency of the mammalian neocortex, with some overlap

42
00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.370
between days. Keep the rule.

43
00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:37.070
When a neuron is born predicts where it settles.
